# HOW MUCH OF EACH CONTRACT'S ROSTER THE POPULATION REACHES ON ITS OWN. One row per contract
# in reports/integrity.sqlc: the subjects it declares, and the subjects some row other than the
# roster's own puts into the produced side.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc STATES THE PRINCIPLE FOR ONE SIDE AND THIS MEASURES THE
#    OTHER. "A contract must not be derived from what it governs", and examples/compositions/main.rs
#    asserts it: every roster here is a bare VALUES literal. Nothing says the converse, and some
#    of the populations below take their subject's NAME from the very roster they are compared
#    against: `checks/all.sqlc` reads `r.rule`, `arithmetic/all.sqlc` reads `a.site`,
#    `algebra/all.sqlc` reads `a.law`. examples/soundness/main.rs §5 prints which, and gates it.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THAT IS A TRADE RATHER THAN A DEFECT, WHICH IS WHY IT WANTS A NUMBER. The roster join
#    is what buys ⛔ VACUOUS: a rule with nothing to examine still emits a row, so the verdict
#    that matters most is a row a reader can see rather than a silence. The price is that the
#    subject is then on both sides of the difference by construction. `diagrams/catalogue.sqlc`
#    pays no such price and could not: its population is `information_schema`, written by
#    Postgres, and `diagrams/expected.sqlc` names each law in its own arm as a literal.
#
# ⛔⛔ WHAT THE SHORTFALL IS AND IS NOT. A subject counted short is one the population reached
#    with nothing of its own, so every branch of the difference sees it only through the roster.
#    ⛔ It is NOT a count of failures a guard would miss; read that way it imports a perturbation
#    this relation never names. Delete an arm from a population outright and branch one reports
#    that subject whether it was short here or not.
#
# ⛔ SQL CANNOT SEE WHICH POPULATIONS TAKE THEIR KEY FROM THEIR ROSTER, only where a corpus
#   happens to make it visible. A contract built exactly like `rules` shows no shortfall here
#   while its population is complete, so a full column is not evidence of a second witness.
#   That property is a fact about the TEMPLATE and examples/soundness/main.rs §5 is where it lives.
#
# ⛔ THE SUBJECT COUNT IS DISTINCT ON THE KEY BECAUSE `EXCEPT` IS. algebra/roster.sqlc files
#   more rows than it has law strings, its own `multiplicity` column calls that collapse
#   intended, and the difference sees the strings. Counting roster ROWS here would report a
#   denominator the guard does not have; `cargo run --example soundness` prints both live.
#
# ⛔ COUNTS ARE NOT WRITTEN IN THIS HEADER. Run it; the corpus moves and a sentence does not.
-- every contract reports/integrity.sqlc differences, each population counted back against its roster.
SELECT 'rules' AS contract,
       count(DISTINCT r.rule)                            AS subjects,
       count(DISTINCT r.rule) FILTER (WHERE p.reaches)   AS witnessed
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.rule, bool_or(c.violates IS NOT NULL) AS reaches
    FROM ( :compose(checks/all.sqlc) ) c
    GROUP BY c.rule
) p ON p.rule = r.rule
UNION ALL
SELECT 'arithmetic',
       count(DISTINCT a.site),
       count(DISTINCT a.site) FILTER (WHERE q.reaches)
FROM      (
    :compose(arithmetic/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT z.site, bool_or(z.verdict IS NOT NULL) AS reaches
    FROM ( :compose(arithmetic/all.sqlc) ) z
    GROUP BY z.site
) q ON q.site = a.site
UNION ALL
SELECT 'algebra',
       count(DISTINCT g.law),
       count(DISTINCT g.law) FILTER (WHERE v.reaches)
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) g
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT w.law, bool_or(w.holds IS NOT NULL) AS reaches
    FROM ( :compose(algebra/all.sqlc) ) w
    GROUP BY w.law
) v ON v.law = g.law
UNION ALL
SELECT 'diagrams',
       count(DISTINCT d.object),
       count(DISTINCT d.object) FILTER (WHERE k.object IS NOT NULL)
FROM      (
    :compose(diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc)
) d
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(diagrams/catalogue.sqlc)
) k ON k.object = d.object
UNION ALL
SELECT 'diagram laws',
       count(DISTINCT l.slug),
       count(DISTINCT l.slug) FILTER (WHERE e.slug IS NOT NULL)
FROM      (
    :compose(diagrams/roster.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(diagrams/expected.sqlc)
) e ON e.slug = l.slug
